<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=transatlantic-telegraph-cable">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transatlantic telegraph cable</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> cables were undersea cables laid across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> for telegraph communication between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The Atlantic Telegraph Company, led by Cyrus West Field, laid the first from Valentia Island off Ireland to Trinity Bay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, and it carried its first messages on August 16, 1858, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s congratulations to President James Buchanan, but it failed after three weeks. Cables laid from the SS Great Eastern in 1865 and 1866 proved durable, and since 1866 the continents have been linked, so that a message could be answered on the same day rather than after weeks by ship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transatlantic-telegraph-cable.jpg" alt="Transatlantic telegraph cable" width="330" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Charles Dudley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landing_of_the_Atlantic_Cable_of_1866,_Heart's_Content,_Newfoundland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
